    "case_id": "596",
    "case_summary": "A 48-year-old African American woman was admitted with marked hypercalcemia and impaired renal function. Her only symptom was unexpected weight loss over 3 months. Medical history included diabetes (developed after acute pancreatitis 2 months prior), hypertension, and remote stroke. Home medications included carvedilol, baby aspirin, simvastatin, ergocalciferol, and insulin twice daily. Family history was significant for a maternal aunt with multiple myeloma.\n\nPhysical examination revealed a moderately obese female with normal vital signs. Her chest was clear, heart sounds were normal, jugular venous pressure was not elevated, and abdominal examination was unremarkable. There was a trace of peripheral edema below the knees bilaterally.\n\n- Laboratory Tests (Initial):    - Creatinine: 189 umol/L    - Calcium: 3.7 mmol/L    - Intact parathyroid hormone: 22 ng/L    - 25-vitamin D: 59.9 nmol/L    - Hemoglobin: 124 g/L  - Laboratory Tests (After volume resuscitation):    - Potassium: 3.1 mmol/L    - Bicarbonate: 17 mmol/L    - Sodium: 144 mmol/L    - Chloride: 118 mmol/L    - Creatinine: 159 umol/L    - Urea: 5 mmol/L    - Calcium: 3 mmol/L    - Magnesium: 0.7 mmol/L    - Phosphorus: 0.48 mmol/L    - Hemoglobin: 98 g/L  - Imaging:    - Chest X-ray: Normal (1 day after admission)      - Biopsy:    Image Title: Renal biopsy (Jones Silver stain)    Image Description: A glomerulus with Bowman's capsule outlined by the stain and surrounded by noncaseating granulomata",
